  "generated_text": "To find the phase shift of the graph of the function \\( y = \\sin (3x - \\pi) \\), we need to analyze the argument of the sine function. The general form of a sine function with a phase shift is:\n\n\\[ y = \\sin(bx - c) \\]\n\nwhere \\( a \\) is the amplitude, \\( b \\) affects the period, and \\( c \\) determines the phase shift.\n\nIn the given function \\( y = \\sin (3x - \\pi) \\), we can identify:\n- \\( b = 3 \\)\n- \\( c = \\pi \\)\n\nThe phase shift is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Phase shift} = \\frac{c}{b} \\]\n\nSubstituting the values of \\( c \\) and \\( b \\):\n\n\\[ \\text{Phase shift} = \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\]\n\nSince the argument is \\( bx - \\pi \\), the phase shift is to the right.\n\nTherefore, the phase shift of the graph of \\( y = \\sin (3x - \\pi) \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{3}} \\]",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[pi/3, '\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{3}']",
    "gold_answer": "Since the graph of $y = \\sin (3x - \\pi)$ is the same as the graph of $y = \\sin 3x$ shifted $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ units to the right, the phase shift is $\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}.$\n\n[asy]import TrigMacros;\n\nsize(400);\n\nreal g(real x)\n{\n\treturn sin(3*x - pi);\n}\n\nreal f(real x)\n{\n\treturn sin(3*x);\n}\n\ndraw(graph(g,-2*pi,2*pi,n=700,join=operator ..),red);\ndraw(graph(f,-2*pi,2*pi,n=700,join=operator ..));\ntrig_axes(-2*pi,2*pi,-2,2,pi/2,1);\nlayer();\nrm_trig_labels(-4,4, 2);\n[/asy]\n\nNote that we can also shift the graph of $y = \\sin 3x$ $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ units to the left, so an answer of $\\boxed{-\\frac{\\pi}{3}}$ is also acceptable.",
    "is_correct": false
  },
